WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.760
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér. Dr. Papers.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.220
Today we are going to look at an incredible&nbsp;
way to supercharge Stable Diffusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:17.700
which is a free an open text to image&nbsp;
AI. Text goes in, beautiful images come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.340
out. We all know that. However, this&nbsp;
is ControlNet, a new neural network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:29.100
structure that helps us give it additional&nbsp;
inputs. What does that mean? More control.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:35.820
For instance, we can provide just the edges&nbsp;
of an input image. This can be a rough sketch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:42.480
or edges extracted from a real photo,&nbsp;
and bam, we get a beautiful image with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.000
exactly that creature and framing.&nbsp;
Wow, that is exactly what I want!

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.380
We can also provide this thing&nbsp;
that we call a boundary map,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.200
and ControlNet is able to follow that too.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.060
Do you know what else also works?&nbsp;
Segmentation maps. This is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.100
a rough draft of where different&nbsp;
objects should be, and my goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:11.940
out comes a beautiful piece of landscape&nbsp;
and architecture, or even an interior. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.940
just look at that. All this came from these&nbsp;
really coarse, poor images. And don’t forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:24.900
this works both ways, so we can even give&nbsp;
the AI a photo, ask for the segmentation map,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.540
and then add a prompt to make it a&nbsp;
painting, remove a tree or anything else.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:33.960
Pose works too. A stickman goes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.060
and a photorealistic image&nbsp;
comes out. That is incredible.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.140
So, what else can we do with ControlNet? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.460
after looking at these 4 amazing&nbsp;
examples that I am about to show you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:52.080
by the end of this video, you will see&nbsp;
that a more appropriate question would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.660
be what can’t we do with it. I will also tell&nbsp;
you whether you can try it or not in a moment.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:04.560
Now hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because this is going to be insane. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:12.900
with ControlNet, we can even transform ourselves.&nbsp;
In goes a dancing video of us, and out comes this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.000
Is this really happening? I cannot&nbsp;
believe this. Just look at how good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.520
the temporal coherence is! Yes, there are&nbsp;
some unexpected changes from image to image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:29.280
a little flickering, but this is improving&nbsp;
so fast, two more papers down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.160
I bet my papers that no one is going to&nbsp;
be able to tell. Just imagine how artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.460
and even non-artists will be able to unleash their&nbsp;
creativity with this. Don’t have an expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:48.960
camera to record this? Not a problem. No access to&nbsp;
expensive lighting equipment? Not a problem. Heck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.900
you don’t even need to be at the location,&nbsp;
it can be synthesized! This is incredible.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:01.080
Two, we can even create a group photo,&nbsp;
and we can all enter a virtual world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.360
where we can be recreated with the&nbsp;
same pose, character by character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:13.620
How cool is that? So this was cartooning.&nbsp;
And, are you thinking what I am thinking?

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.140
Oh yes, three, decartooning&nbsp;
is also possible. This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:25.920
we can make cartoons and even our own drawn&nbsp;
characters come to life. I really like how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.380
the skirt has been reimagined in a way that&nbsp;
it could really exist in the real world.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:38.760
Four, Weights and Biases also asked the AI to&nbsp;
reimagine their logo. And it did not disappoint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:45.060
Some of these are really imaginative. And wait,&nbsp;
this is not just style transfer. Look at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.100
animation example where the original real&nbsp;
footage is used as a control signal. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:56.460
these indeed look like style transfer into&nbsp;
a watercolor or oil painting, but now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.080
look! It can truly reimagine the footage. Amazing!

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.480
And the best part about ControlNet&nbsp;
is whenever I am working with a tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.780
like this I feel more satisfied, and more&nbsp;
strongly involved in the creative process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:18.720
I am not just prompting, I feel like I&nbsp;
am at least partly creating something.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.540
I also feel like people are sleeping on this&nbsp;
paper. They don’t yet know that the world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:30.420
digital art as we know it will change very&nbsp;
quickly. More of us will have access to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:36.240
fantastic tools that we could never afford&nbsp;
to. And now it is available for all of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.400
for free! The possibilities truly&nbsp;
make my head spin. And this is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.960
one paper. And this is a technique&nbsp;
that is free and open to everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.620
we build this together, so you Fellow Scholars&nbsp;
are all welcome to contribute prompts, code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:59.160
experiments, anything. This gives power to&nbsp;
the people for free, that's what I want. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:04.860
I put a link to it in the video description&nbsp;
and to a guide too, so you can try it for free.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:11.520
And one more interesting tidbit from the paper&nbsp;
- this neural network can even work with smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:19.620
datasets, so if we have less than 50 thousand&nbsp;
images, that is completely fine, wow, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:26.520
can scale to billions of images if we so desire.&nbsp;
A big thanks for the Stable Diffusion subreddit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:33.960
as they host a ton of amazing artistic works with&nbsp;
ControlNet. And now, let the experiments begin!

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.700
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

